<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Artemisium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/480-bce">480 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Artemisium was a series of naval engagements fought over three days in August 480 BCE off the northern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, at the same time as the land battle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a>. The Greek fleet under the Spartan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurybiades">Eurybiades</a>, with the Athenian contingent led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, held the Persian navy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> to an indecisive result, then withdrew south when Thermopylae fell, regrouping for the victory at Salamis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-artemisium.jpg" alt="Battle of Artemisium" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Thermopylae_and_movements_to_Salamis,_480_BC.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Persian invasion of 480 BCE was a delayed response to the defeat at Marathon. The Athenians, guided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a>, had decided in 482 BCE to build a large fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>, and a congress of city states at Corinth formed an alliance. After a first plan to hold the Vale of Tempe was abandoned, Themistocles proposed that the allies block the Persian army at the pass of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and the Persian navy at the Straits of Artemisium. The Allied fleet sailed to Cape Artemisium and waited. It was led by the Spartan Eurybiades, since the Athenians, who had asked for command, yielded it to preserve unity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Approaching the strait, the Persian navy was caught in a gale off Magnesia that wrecked about a third of its ships. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> then sent 200 ships round <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> to trap the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, and a second storm wrecked them. On the first day the Persians attacked late, and about 30 of their ships were captured or sunk. On the second day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> destroyed a patrol of Cilician ships.</p><p>On the third day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> formed a semicircle to enclose the Greek fleet, and the fleets fought until nightfall with roughly equal losses. The smaller Allied fleet could scarcely afford them, and half the Athenian ships were damaged or lost. News then came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> that the Allied rearguard had been destroyed. Since holding Artemisium no longer served a purpose, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> evacuated at once.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> sailed on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histiaea">Histiaea</a> and sacked the surrounding region, while the Allied fleet withdrew to Salamis to help evacuate the remaining Athenians. The Persian army took the evacuated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and the Persians were later defeated at Salamis. Xerxes withdrew to Asia, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a>, and the Greek victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> in August 479 BCE ended the invasion.</p><p>Taken by itself the battle was indecisive: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> did not defeat the Persian navy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> did not destroy the Greek fleet. In the wider war it mattered because the Allies showed themselves they could stand up to the Persians, the crews gained experience and the admirals learned how the Persian fleet fought. The pre-battle events had also reduced the Persian fleet, so that the odds at Salamis were not overwhelming. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> called Artemisium the place where the sons of the Athenians laid the shining foundation-stone of freedom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Artemisium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
